so in this video i'm gonna be answering  a pretty common question do i need to be  good at math to become an architect math  is involved in every step of an  architecture project and i'd say it does  play a pretty big part in turning a  concept into a real building but while  architects do use math on a daily basis  it's not exactly in the way that most  people think  [Music]  hey guys welcome back to the channel my  name is dami and i'm an architect in  beautiful vancouver bc and today we're  going to talk about a couple different  ways we use math to design buildings and  make them come to life this video is  sponsored by autodesk click on the  description to get a free 30-day trial  probably the most common use of math for  us is when calculating the area so  whether we're designing a new building  or renovating an existing space we need  to show the client how much area we're  creating or gaining or losing because if  this math doesn't work out chances are  the project won't go through so you get  the overall gross floor area of the  building by multiplying the building's  length and width and then for each space  that you create within the building but  most architects these days use a bim  modeling system and uh personally i use  revit and actually the modeling software  does most of these basic calculations  for you meaning you  most likely never have to measure  dimensions or calculate areas by hand  however we do still manually calculate  certain things like when we're massing  up the building  the building's massing or the form is  driven by what we call a floor space  ratio and pretty much every real  building that you design  starts with this constraint most cities  or jurisdictions will have regulations  and guidance on how big or how dense or  how tall you can build on a particular  site this basically ensures that you  don't get any buildings that are totally  out of scale from its context typically  every property has what you call a floor  space ratio or fsr for short and that's  a number it's a ratio of a building's  total gross floor area to the size of  the land that the building's on so this  measures the density of the site and in  order for you to figure out how much  floor area you can build you take the  area of your site  and multiply it by the fsr so let's say  your property is 45 meters by 35 meters  and that would be  1575 square meters  and let's say you have an fsr of  3.03 that means you can build up to 47  25 square meters where it becomes kind  of interesting is when you combine this  with other constraints for your project  it can be legal constraints like  setbacks or height limits  or it can be physical constraints like  views or topography or soil conditions  or it could even be budgetary  constraints for example maybe the client  only has money to build one story of the  building  so taking all of this into consideration  you massage the massing of the building  however  if this is a commercial building owner  like a developer this whole thing is  pretty straightforward they're going to  want to maximize the fsr so that they  can build more  and they can sell more units so that  they can make more profit so let's go  back to my example for a sec the 45 by  35 meter site with the fsr of 3 and the  gfa of 47.25 square meters and let's say  your zoning regulations say you need to  set back 6 meters at the front and 2  meter on the sides the building's  footprint is going to get smaller  meaning if you want to achieve that 47  25 square meters you're gonna have to go  taller and from here you can determine  the height and the number of stories for  your building you're also going to want  to factor in any neighboring buildings  or view corridors or trees or anything  that might suggest a different shape for  your building you also want to factor in  the market unit sizes we want to make  sure that the units we're creating is  kind of in the ballpark of what's  marketable for the developer so as  you're sculpting your building based on  some of these constraints you're gonna  be adjusting these areas as well now if  your zoning has a height limit or  setback that doesn't allow you to do  what you want that's when you get into  negotiations with the city into a  process called rezoning all these  setbacks and rules are guidelines but if  you can make a really strong argument  for why your building can benefit the  neighborhood by deviating from this  guideline the city or the township if  they're open-minded is going to want to  work with you to help you make this  happen and they're going to do this by  helping you re-zone the site with a  different set of rules all of this  requires you to do some basic math um  and most of it can be done with a  calculator or a simple spreadsheet one  of the other misconceptions about  architects is that we work alone and  that we are these lone creatives  drafting late into the night but  we simply can't do our work without  collaborating with other professionals  bim collaborate pro is a tool that makes  this process a lot more efficient with  the ability to seamlessly coordinate  your model with your team within a very  secure common data environment it makes  it easy to give feedback resolve issues  and just get a better overall view of  your project so that your team can stay  on track together by connecting your  model with models from other disciplines  it automatically detects where clashes  could happen and it helps you resolve  them typically the architect is  responsible for manually coordinating  these elements but this is way more  accurate and it saves us a lot of time  issues can be marked directly in the  model which is then turned into a simple  list that you can work through this  means that you can easily leave notes or  comments directly in the model so that  you or your team can resolve it and  nothing gets missed click on the  description to get a free 30-day trial  so the next area where we use math is  for high level budgeting or costing so  when we design buildings we want to make  sure that we're not designing something  that's going to be insanely expensive so  we will typically have check-in points  throughout the project with a cost  estimator to make sure that we're kind  of in the ballpark of the client's  budget and we do costing at different  points of a project and they get more  and more detailed as we progress the  design for example we'll do one at the  schematic design stage we'll also do one  at the design development stage and  we'll do one after the construction  documents as we fine-tune the costing  we go through the list and try to  identify areas that could  be a cost savings to the client and that  can range from  changing the structure or the shape of  your building  if it's earlier on in the project all  the way down to the finishes that you  pick and although the costing of the  building is done by a cost estimator the  architect does review all of those  numbers  and make sure that  all of those areas that they've  calculated based on are the correct  areas they are specialists in what they  do so typically they know what's best  and they know what they're costing but  sometimes you might have found a product  that kind of does the same job but it's  like half the cost and in that case you  can recommend that to the cost estimator  this is also just pretty basic  multiplication and  just learning to use spreadsheets  and finally structure so when people  think about math and architecture  typically they think about architects  calculating structural loads but in  reality  we don't really do that  and even if we wanted to  i don't think insurance companies would  like that very much because architects  already take on so much liability for  other work so typically  we work with structural engineers who  design the structure and they take on  that responsibility and that liability  for the calculations for us it's more  about the high level understanding of  structures and having some basic  conceptual framework for what we're  designing and we just need to focus on  areas that have an impact on the design  for example if you wanted to design the  building to cantilever you know that  it's going to need some beefy structural  members under here so make sure you  leave some room for that you also want  to make sure your walls are aligned to  the structural grids as much as possible  or maybe you want to use  exposed wood beams for aesthetic or  sustainability purposes once you have  those ideas you just work with your  structural engineer to find the exact  sizing and spacing of those members and  the overall system  so this is generally the extent of how  we use math in our day to day but  we do  borrow a lot of mathematical principles  for design for example the golden ratio  angles and proportions  and understanding these principles is  gonna make you a better architect and a  designer so i hope this gave you guys  some insights into how we use math it's  really not a lot of math and it should  never be the thing that hinders you from  going into architecture however there  are specific skills that you should  consider before going into architecture  and if you're interested in learning  about those check out my video right  here i also have a video where i talk  about the realities of going into  construction and other skills that would  help you become a more successful  architect i'll leave that video right  here as well i also have a monthly  newsletter where i share my insights  things i'm learning reading or listening  to  as well as a discord channel where you  can chat with other people in this  amazing community if you want to support  the channel and listen in on interviews  by me with other industry professionals  as well as access to other perks i have  membership tiers that you can join and  i'll leave links to all of this in the  description of the video and with that  i'll see you guys in the next video  [Music]  you

